WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.850
Film Courage: What do you think makes you
different from the other screenwriters who

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:08.029
have come to Hollywood during the same time,
where you've been able to make a career at

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:09.830
this and possibly they haven't?

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:14.080
Jim Agnew, Screenwriter: I think understanding
the business because I was in the business

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:21.140
part of it first and producing and just knowing
how distribution works knowing what they know,

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:26.470
how actors sell films and things like that
because just you can kind of can filter it

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.449
through a different kind of lens where it's
like okay this is what people are looking

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:34.300
for because I've been to AFM [American Film
Market], I've been to Cannes, I've been to

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.900
Sundance on the business side of it so you
see all those posters, you see what kind of

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:43.380
movies are trying to put together and you
also see how many movies are being put together

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.740
you know it's like astronomical if you go
to AFM how many films are tens of thousands

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:54.400
of them and maybe you know a finite number
of those get made but you see a recurring

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.530
theme and you kind of understand how these
people work you know and it just depends on

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:04.079
on what kind of like you know level you're
at to studio level is completely different

00:01:04.079 --> 00:01:08.219
i'm talking more about the indie world you
know but breaking into the studio world is

00:01:08.219 --> 00:01:12.150
very difficult as well he said you have to
work your way up on that so i think that gave

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:17.340
me advantage that was just understanding um
the business and i can also put myself in

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.369
the mindset of a producer when i'm writing
something and i know okay the producer's not

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:27.929
gonna like that but they'll like this so that
helps where someone else may have come out

00:01:27.929 --> 00:01:33.759
but they had no prior film industry experience
and so even though it's a very personalized

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:39.509
story it just won't work because it doesn't
tick these different boxes exactly and like

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:43.969
who's going to be like like we talk about
the the movie about your uncle who runs a

00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:49.520
you know a pawn shop you know what actor is
dying to play that part that can finance a

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.179
film and then that turns into a whole other
conversation altogether because there's so

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:57.759
few actors at certain levels that can finance
a film that's the reason you see so many Bruce

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:02.490
Willis movies, Nick Cage movies, you know
John Travolta movies there's not a big pool

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:08.769
of people that can justify people spending
five 10 20 million dollars so that's part

00:02:08.769 --> 00:02:14.230
understanding that's a big part of it as well
how does genre factor in getting a movie made

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:19.430
well this is a very important thing to remember
is that the reason you see so many action

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:25.340
films on uh Netflix and things like that it's
because action films and thrillers translate

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:30.819
internationally if you want to make a comedy
and it's a comedy about a bunch of kids in

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:35.349
New Jersey and and you know they're going
through their last summer before they graduate

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:39.379
high school and there's a bunch of jokes about
you know American kids in jersey that's not

00:02:39.379 --> 00:02:43.720
going to translate in like Malaysia they're
not going to understand it so those movies

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:49.870
have no very little international value but
now action and thrillers sci-fi and horror

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:53.799
is like an international language that's why
you see so many of those films because they

00:02:53.799 --> 00:02:57.879
can sell not only in America but all around
the world and that's also something you need

00:02:57.879 --> 00:03:03.209
to keep in mind if you have this small intimate
you know film that you want to make about

00:03:03.209 --> 00:03:08.659
the screenplay that's about you know four
sisters in Ohio and it's a comedy drama it's

00:03:08.659 --> 00:03:14.370
like you've got a very small audience for
that so you know that's why producers don't

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:17.600
like things like that and that's why when
you look you can see so many of those action

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.069
thrillers on Netflix because they make money
internationally but what if it's in someone's

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:27.459
heart i don't mean to sound so cheesy but
what if it's what if it's in someone's DNA

00:03:27.459 --> 00:03:32.430
that they just want to make a coming-of-age
story and to them they have to get that story

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:38.620
out there and they want to finish it well
i mean if if you're a screenwriter like that

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:43.650
that's got that story i think you should become
a filmmaker if it's that that story is like

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:48.439
figure out a way to make the ten 10 000 version
of that or the 50 000 version of it and make

00:03:48.439 --> 00:03:53.799
it you know that's that's the thing is like
you've probably got a better chance of like

00:03:53.799 --> 00:03:57.890
cobbling together your friends and making
a decent movie of that and maybe getting in

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:02.580
some film festivals getting noticed like that
than just sending your script out to people

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.200
so that's what i would suggest you know now
that takes money and resources but if that's

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.630
your passion project you know spend a few
years you know putting together all those

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:15.170
resources or figure out a way to shoot it
there's you know there's great films that

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:18.690
have been made for ten thousand dollars you
know you just have to understand how filmmaking

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:23.890
works that's a whole nother conversation altogether
but you can do it i think we've already touched

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:27.990
on this but is it correct that you didn't
get an agent until you sold your first script

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:34.470
i had a manager and then um when we sold the
second script there was an article about the

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:40.000
Hollywood reporter and then literally that
next morning there was like 10 agents called

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.530
now you don't always need to here's the thing
is agents are great but they're not a magic

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:49.060
bullet for your career either you know they
do things for you and managers do too but

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.090
you have to do a lot on your own you have
to meet producers you have to get out there

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:57.880
you have to do things um and i think actually
part of that what got the ball rolling on

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.710
that that film was was people really dug the
script but i think the reason we got involved

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:05.970
with those producers because i'm forgetting
some of this stuff is a friend of mine who

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:11.080
had worked with me on a film that i had produced
who was a production coordinator was now working

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:14.870
for them as a development exec and she was
like you should check out their script so

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:20.840
it all kind of tied together from one thing
into another so it's all about those relationships

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:26.500
again what was the title of the article don't
remember just it was one of those generic

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:32.090
sold sold a script you know but back to the
agent manager thing it's like you know they're

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:37.979
great to have but they they work you know
they only have so much resources to give you

00:05:37.979 --> 00:05:42.310
you can't like rely on them for everything
even if they care and they they take the time

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:46.900
to read but they have 20 other clients so
you have to like really understand you know

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:52.050
what how to use them as well have a relationship
where because they their bottom they have

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:56.199
a bottom line you know they have to make money
too so a lot of people think oh i just want

00:05:56.199 --> 00:05:59.800
an agent manager but you have to think what
are you providing them are you selling things

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:04.110
do you have things you can sell you know are
they going to take you on as a client and

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:09.430
build your career so where are you in the
process as well what do you provide for them

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:15.840
and so when you got those calls the next day
how did you know which person you wanted to

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.580
go with or or even entertain going with you
you have you have meetings and you meet with

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:25.210
them and then you kind of just decide you
know who's who's the correct person you know

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:30.349
based on based on instinct and and also to
um yeah chemistry and and also the manager

00:06:30.349 --> 00:06:34.170
will kind of push you in that right direction
too we had a manager at the time and they

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:38.970
were like hey you know our manager said i've
got some other clients with with him and you

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:44.889
know so it's all very there's a lot of synergy
there when you got those calls and i'm assuming

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:48.420
it wasn't like you woke up and then your phone's
buzzing it's like one of those no it's not

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:53.240
like that hey jim this is so you know so but
how did were you like this is happening like

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:58.570
i've i've made it i mean it felt good to have
an agent but it felt good to have a manager

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:02.680
too but you quickly learned that like you
know you've got to do the work it's like okay

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:07.879
well you sold something that you know they
weren't part of so they didn't get commission

00:07:07.879 --> 00:07:11.699
so they're like what's next you know and so
it's like you've got to get back on the treadmill

00:07:11.699 --> 00:07:14.150
and start like figuring things out and doing
things.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:14.569
